--TEST--
`Core\Test::assertEquals` compares an object only through `Comparable`, and its refusal names the class and the two members that do work on it -- `rule:testing/assertions-are-typed`, `rule:classes/ordering-lowers-to-compare-to`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:classes/comparable` gives an object no ordering but the one its class declares, so the
// equality member built on `compareTo` has an edge exactly where the class has
// none. What is asserted here is not that the edge exists -- that much a bare
// refusal would show -- but that the message is *specific*: it quotes the
// receiver through the same rendering the failure messages use, so it names
// the class, and it names `assertEqualsDeep` and `assertSame` rather than
// leaving a reader with "objects are not supported".
//
// The two rows beside it are what make that true. A class that does implement
// `Comparable` is compared on the line above, and both named members are run
// on the very object the refusal named on the lines below.

class Version implements Comparable {
    public function constructor(public int $n) {}

    public function compareTo(Version $other): int {
        return $this->n - $other->n;
    }
}

final class Cell {
    public function constructor(public string $name) {}
}

int $answered = 0;
int $refused = 0;

// The accepting side of the bound: two distinct allocations agreeing through
// `compareTo` compare equal, and two disagreeing through it fail with an
// ordinary failure message rather than with the refusal below.
Version $two = new Version(2);
Version $also = new Version(2);
Version $five = new Version(5);
try { Core\Test::assertEquals($two, $also); $answered = $answered + 1; } catch (Throwable $ordered) { echo $ordered->message, "\n"; }
try { Core\Test::assertEquals($two, $five); } catch (Throwable $unequal) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $unequal->message, "\n"; }

// The refusing side: a class with no `compareTo`. The class name is in the
// message because the receiver goes through the same `shown` the failure
// messages use.
Cell $held = new Cell("a");
Cell $twin = new Cell("a");
try { Core\Test::assertEquals($held, $twin); } catch (Throwable $unordered) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $unordered->message, "\n"; }

// Both members the refusal names, run on the object it named. Structurally
// the two cells agree, and by identity they do not -- which is the whole
// reason the message names two members and not one.
try { Core\Test::assertEqualsDeep($held, $twin); $answered = $answered + 1; } catch (Throwable $deep) { echo $deep->message, "\n"; }
try { Core\Test::assertSame($held, $held); $answered = $answered + 1; } catch (Throwable $identity) { echo $identity->message, "\n"; }
try { Core\Test::assertSame($held, $twin); } catch (Throwable $apart) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $apart->message, "\n"; }

// Counted, not read off the lines: a member that started answering an
// unordered pair -- by identity, say -- would drop the refusal and add an
// answer, and both halves of this line move.
echo "answered=", $answered, "/3 refused=", $refused, "/3\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Test::assertEquals failed: `$actual` is a `Version`, `$expected` is a `Version`
Core\Test::assertEquals compares an object only through `Comparable`, and a `Cell` declares no `compareTo` — write `Core\Test::assertEqualsDeep` for a structural comparison, or `Core\Test::assertSame` for identity
Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is a `Cell`, `$expected` is a `Cell`
answered=3/3 refused=3/3
